# cv-infra — NVIDIA driver branch pin (decision 2026-07-03-driver-r580-realignment).
#
# WHY: Isaac Sim 5.1.0 (kit 107.3.3) deterministically segfaults in the RTX
# renderer (librtx.scenedb.plugin.so, ~50ms after "app ready") on the R595
# driver branch — confirmed upstream as a known issue with no workaround. The
# branch Isaac 5.1.0 is certified against is R580 (LTSB, EOL 2028-06). This
# file (a) holds every R580 userspace component at the build the realignment
# actually installed and (b) hard-blocks the newer branches so a routine
# `apt upgrade` / unattended-upgrades can never re-ascend past R580 (the
# original incident: a floor-only ">= 580.65.06" assert admitted 595.71.05).
#
# Canonical copy: cv-infra-workspace/scripts/workstation_setup/apt-preferences-cv-infra-nvidia-r580
# Deployed to:    /etc/apt/preferences.d/cv-infra-nvidia-r580 (by realign_driver_r580.sh)
#
# ★ THIS IS A TEMPLATE, NOT A DEPLOYABLE FILE. Stanza 1 carries the placeholder
# @CV_DRIVER_TARGET_UPSTREAM@, which realign_driver_r580.sh substitutes with the
# build it is installing on THAT host (and it verifies the substitution happened).
# Reason: two hosts, two R580 builds — 580.159.03 (Ubuntu noble archive, etri6000,
# realigned 2026-07-03) and 580.178.04 (CEO local jammy host, realigned 2026-08-18).
# Hardcoding either one makes the file wrong on the other host: at Pin-Priority 1001
# apt would try to DOWNGRADE a perfectly good R580 build to the other host's build.
# What must be pinned is the BRANCH (the ceiling below), not one machine's patch
# level — and the exact build the operator installed must still be held, so the
# placeholder keeps a hard hold without ever becoming an unbounded `580*` (an
# unbounded hold is a floor without a ceiling *inside* the branch — G-12's shape).
#
# NOTE: per-kernel module packages (linux-modules-nvidia-580-open-<kver>) carry
# KERNEL version numbers, so stanza 1 deliberately does not version-hold them —
# they must track kernel updates; their branch is fixed by the package name.
#
# MAINTENANCE RULE for the negative stanzas: they block by PACKAGE NAME, so each
# newer branch needs its own stanza — a branch that has no stanza is not blocked.
# R590 was missing here and is visible in apt on the jammy host today (measured
# 2026-08-19: `apt-cache search --names-only '^nvidia-driver-[0-9]+'`), i.e. the
# G-12 surface had reopened silently. When a new branch number appears upstream,
# add a stanza. (Un-blocking one requires evidence that Isaac 5.1.0 runs on it.)

# 1) Hold all R580 userspace components at the build this host was realigned to.
#    @CV_DRIVER_TARGET_UPSTREAM@ is substituted at deploy time (see above).
Package: *nvidia*580*
Pin: version @CV_DRIVER_TARGET_UPSTREAM@*
Pin-Priority: 1001

# 2) Never install anything from the R590 branch (uncertified for Isaac 5.1.0).
Package: *nvidia*590*
Pin: version *
Pin-Priority: -1

# 3) Never install anything from the R595 branch (Isaac 5.1.0 RTX segfault).
Package: *nvidia*595*
Pin: version *
Pin-Priority: -1

# 4) Never install anything from the R610 branch (uncertified for Isaac 5.1.0).
Package: *nvidia*610*
Pin: version *
Pin-Priority: -1
